WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.654
Every detection in this
module depends on evidence,

2
00:00:03.654 --> 00:00:06.000
and AD CS produces none by default.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.300
Two settings are needed, and
neither works without the other.

4
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.250
The CA's audit filter selects
which CA operations are audited.

5
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.326
The Audit Certification
Services advanced audit

6
00:00:16.326 --> 00:00:19.987
subcategory lets the operating
system generate the events.

7
00:00:19.987 --> 00:00:25.231
Set the subcategory to Success and Failure
in a dedicated GPO linked to the CA

8
00:00:25.231 --> 00:00:28.836
servers, set the audit filter
to cover every category,

9
00:00:28.836 --> 00:00:32.113
and restart the service
in a maintenance window.

10
00:00:32.113 --> 00:00:35.534
Full auditing includes the
start and stop category,

11
00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:40.149
which can slow restarts on a CA with
a large database, so test that first.

12
00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:42.625
Then watch Microsoft's focused baseline.

13
00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:49.428
4886, 4887 and 4888 are requests received,
issued and denied: issuance volume per

14
00:00:49.428 --> 00:00:52.418
template, every credential issued,

15
00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:56.850
and probing of templates
that should be restricted.

16
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.725
4870 is revocation evidence.

17
00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:07.050
4882 and 4890 are permission and certificate
manager changes, which is ESC7 drift.

18
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.450
4885 is someone changing the audit filter.

19
00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:15.950
4892 is a CA property change,
which catches the ESC6 flag.

20
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:20.088
4896 is rows deleted from the CA database.

21
00:01:20.088 --> 00:01:23.513
4897 is role separation enabled.

22
00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:28.110
Microsoft recommends generating each
event in a nonproduction test before

23
00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.563
writing the collection rule, so an
assumed event doesn't become a blind spot.

24
00:01:32.563 --> 00:01:36.637
Forward through Windows Event
Forwarding to a collector and the SIEM.

25
00:01:36.637 --> 00:01:40.940
Forwarding doesn't stop a full
local log from overwriting evidence,

26
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:45.700
so size the CA's Security log for the
longest plausible collection outage.
